<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Freikorps</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–<a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as German Freikorps</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Freikorps were German volunteer paramilitary units formed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> from demobilised soldiers. They crushed left-wing risings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and fought in the Baltic in 1919, where, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-landeswehr">Baltic Landeswehr</a>, they took Riga from the Latvian Soviet government in May 1919 before turning against the Latvian national government and being defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesis">Cēsis</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/freikorps.jpg" alt="Freikorps" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FreikorpsBerlinStahlhelmM18TuerkischeForm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After 1918 the term Freikorps referred to loosely affiliated paramilitary groups formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> after its defeat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>. Their ranks were mostly demobilised soldiers, young men and conservative nationalists who blamed socialists and communists for Germany's problems. Historians agree that some 500,000 men were formal members, with another 1.5 million taking part informally. Many veterans who could not return to civilian life found a sense of belonging in a military structure that continued the comradeship of the trenches. Others joined from anger at defeat, or to exact revenge on those they held responsible.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert">Friedrich Ebert</a> used the Freikorps against socialist and communist uprisings. Defence Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-noske">Gustav Noske</a> relied on them to suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacist-uprising">Spartacist uprising</a>, which ended with the summary executions of Karl Liebknecht and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg">Rosa Luxemburg</a> on 15 January 1919. In late April 1919 Noske sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> totalling some 30,000 men to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> against the Bavarian Soviet Republic, where they acted with particular brutality: at Perlach on 5 May a dozen men, members of the Social Democratic Party, were shot, and the next day twenty members of a Catholic club were beaten, shot and bayoneted to death.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> the units fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, Silesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, justifying their campaign as defence against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>. The historian Nigel Jones records a massacre of 500 Latvian civilians and the slaughter of some 3,000 people at the capture of Riga. The Iron Division was defeated by the Estonian and Latvian armies at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cesis">Battle of Cēsis</a>. After the failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapp-putsch">Kapp Putsch</a> of March 1920 the army commander Hans von Seeckt removed Freikorps members from the army, and by 1921 only a small core remained.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Many former members went on to the Black Reichswehr, which Seeckt organised in 1923 and later disbanded after a failed putsch at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostrzyn-nad-odra">Küstrin</a>, and to the Nazi Sturmabteilung. Future Nazi figures such as Martin Bormann, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinhard-heydrich">Reinhard Heydrich</a> and Rudolf Höss served in Freikorps units, although recent research finds that former members were no more likely to join Nazi organisations than the average German man. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/night-of-the-long-knives">Night of the Long Knives</a> in 1934, Hitler had Freikorps leaders, among them Hermann Ehrhardt, targeted for killing or arrest.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Freikorps” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The national army was formed during the War of Independence of 1918–1920, when the government fought the Latvian Soviet republic, the Baltic German Landeswehr, <b>German Freikorps</b> units and the Bermontians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1919 the Spartacist uprising was crushed by <b>Freikorps</b> units, and Rosa Luxemburg and Karl Liebknecht were killed after their arrests.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the defeat of the Spartacist uprising, the two communist leaders were seized and killed by <b>Freikorps</b> soldiers.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-15">15 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Government troops and <b>Freikorps</b> units entered Munich on 1-3 May 1919, killing several hundred people, including Landauer.</blockquote><a href="/state/bavarian-soviet-republic">Bavarian Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>On 4 February 1919 the <b>Freikorps</b> Division Gerstenberg, sent by Gustav Noske, stormed Bremen.</blockquote><a href="/state/bremen-soviet-republic">Bremen Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote><b>German Freikorps</b> and the Baltic Landeswehr, with Latvian national units, recaptured Riga on 22 May 1919.</blockquote><a href="/state/latvian-socialist-soviet-republic">Latvian Socialist Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
